I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided tungstate workflow/tooling. I’ll build a candidate ledger before refinement, then use one stable GSAS-II project for comparable screening and the final quantitative fit.

The chemistry is CuO + WO₃ at 700 °C, so CuWO₄ is the principal expectation, but the inventory is deliberately unknown and may include residual oxides or Cu–W–O intermediates. I’m now extracting peak families and searching the frozen COD across every same-formula CuWO₄ entry plus broader Cu–W–O/oxide prototypes before nominating phases.

The observed peak map strongly tracks the triclinic \(P\bar1\) CuWO₄ entry (COD 4000806): its strongest calculated lines match the observed families after an approximately constant \(-0.45^\circ\) specimen/calibration displacement. The monoclinic CuWO₄ polymorph and Cu₂WO₄/Cu₃WO₆ alternatives predict materially different families; I’m retaining them as explicit trial branches rather than discarding them from visual matching alone.

Two additions are already falsified by peak families: Cu₃WO₆ accounts for the otherwise unexplained 15.18°, 19.80°, 32.51°, 33.77°, 49.67°, 57.60°, and 63.28° lines, while a monoclinic WO₃ family accounts for the strong 22.67°/23.07°/23.89° triplet and 28.14°/28.48° pair. The two-phase model lowers Rwp from 31.6% to 25.5%; the three-phase comparison is still refining.

The full same-formula WO₃ screen separates three closely related low-symmetry models from the tetragonal/hexagonal/orthorhombic alternatives. COD 2311041 (\(P2_1/c\), nonstandard cell) is best under the common protocol at Rwp 9.06%, ahead of the triclinic analogue (9.30%) and alternate monoclinic setting (10.28%); all other stable trials are ≥18.4%. I’m carrying the top three as the structural-uncertainty shortlist while refining only the chemically economical \(P2_1/c\) model first.

The first bounded-coordinate trial exposed a GSAS-II parameter-convention trap: coordinate limits were interpreted as absolute fractional coordinates, not displacements, and the branch subsequently collapsed. Per the requested discipline, I’m rolling that branch back to the valid pre-coordinate checkpoint and treating it as unknown—not as evidence against the structure. I’ll retry with atom-specific absolute bounds centered on each published coordinate.

The rollback is now physically valid: all CIF geometries pass, ADPs are positive, and the profile uses nonnegative widths (Rwp 6.27%). The unconstrained oxygen refinements and boundary-hitting coordinate/ADP branches have been excluded. I’m now screening the only remaining likely intensity correction—one March–Dollase term at a time for each phase and each allowed axis—against this same rollback.

The three-phase inventory is now stable: triclinic CuWO₄, cubic Cu₃WO₆, and monoclinic WO₃. I’m at the final model-selection stage—checking whether any single profile correction is genuinely supported, then I’ll replay the exact GSAS-II project and export all deliverables from that replayed model.

The roughness correction lowered Rwp slightly, but one parameter ran exactly to its allowed boundary, so I’m rejecting it as unsupported. I’m now comparing six one-parameter, phase-specific broadening trials plus the axial-divergence term against the same clean project; this is the last nuisance-model screen.

One apparently strong size improvement exposed a profile-decomposition issue: the common instrumental Lorentzian term went slightly negative, so that trial is not physically admissible as-is. I’m rebuilding the baseline with nonnegative instrumental broadening and negligible phase-size broadening, then retesting phase sizes; this separates actual phase broadening from the arbitrary screening defaults.

With a nonnegative profile gauge, CuWO₄’s smaller effective domain size is reproducible (about 0.112 µm) and converges cleanly; it drops Rwp from 6.50% to 5.00%. The other single-phase trials are much weaker or boundary-limited. I’m now testing whether any second isotropic term adds stable information on top of that supported CuWO₄ broadening.

The fully isotropic model is physically reasonable and reaches Rwp ≈4.62%, but repeated least-squares blocks reveal a numerical instability in the oblique WO₃ metric (two reciprocal-cell terms are ~89% correlated). I’m freezing that already-refined WO₃ cell and reconverging the remaining parameters; the submitted model will use the stable rollback if this removes the oscillation.

Freezing WO₃ confirms the model’s parameters are usable, but GSAS-II’s cached profile was slightly optimistic: a fresh calculation gives Rwp ≈4.70%. I’ll use the independently regenerated profile—not the stale cache—for the final statistics. In parallel, I’m closing the candidate ledger with the alternate CuWO₄ polymorph, Cu₂WO₄, and both CuO precursor references under the same screening protocol.

The largest remaining positive/negative residual pairs are peak-position asymmetries, not missing reflections. The fitted constant zero (−0.480°) is outside the stated calibration range and is exactly the signature expected when specimen height is being absorbed into a constant offset. I’m now separating zero offset from Bragg–Brentano specimen displacement with their physical bounds; this should be much more consequential than further coordinate freedom.

The displacement refinements are computationally slower because every trial regenerates reflection positions across the full 8,301-point scan. All three bounded initializations are still running; I’m comparing them to ensure the result is not an initialization artifact.

The joint zero/height refinements were ill-conditioned and did not complete, so those branches are “unknown,” not evidence against displacement. I stopped them and am switching to the falsifiable version of the test: hold zero at three plausible calibration values and refine only specimen height, then compare the converged branches.

The fixed-zero branches are progressing but remain expensive; the slowdown is intrinsic to GSAS-II’s specimen-shift derivative, which repeatedly rebuilds peak positions. I’m letting these bounded one-parameter branches finish because the current residual pattern makes this test scientifically decisive.

The direct shift refinements proved numerically unsafe: one branch destroyed a cubic cell and the others stalled, so none will be retained. I’m replacing derivative-based refinement with a read-only fixed grid along the physically equivalent zero/height combinations; each point will be independently replayed, which avoids corrupting structural parameters.

The fixed grid shows that forcing a large specimen displacement onto cells already refined with a constant zero is much worse (best grid Rwp 11.1%), so it cannot be adopted directly. One clean test remains: start exactly at the current profile (shift 0, zero −0.47984°) and refine height alone. That avoids the discontinuous jump that destabilized the earlier branches.

The local height derivative made no accepted step, and roughness again hit a bound with 99.5% parameter correlation, so the simpler zero-only model remains. I’m moving to the final structural check: bounded, symmetry-allowed coordinate tests one phase at a time—CuWO₄ all sites, and heavy atoms only for the two minor phases—while keeping the validated profile model fixed in form.

The bounded coordinate tests do not survive independent replay: CuWO₄ coordinates actually worsen Rwp from 4.623% to 4.651%, while the minor-phase shifts are negligible. I’m retaining the simpler regularized structures. The model is now final: three phases, no texture/roughness/height term, and a replay with no physical-review flags. I’m exporting the exact replayed CIFs and profile now.

